  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nToday-tonight... Upper level high pressure will remain in place \nacross the Great Plains as an upper level trough continues to push \nfarther offshore from the NE US coast with its axis sliding down the \nFlorida peninsula into central Florida. Short wave energy aloft will \ncontinue to traverse across the Florida peninsula through the day, \naiding convection this afternoon and early evening. At the surface, \nhigh pressure is surpressed to our south and east and a weak frontal \nboundary is situated across north Florida and is very slowly \nshifting southward. This frontal boundary may push into \nnorth/central Florida tonight, but confidence remains low. Locally, \nlight westerly winds will shift onshore in the afternoon with the \nformation of the east coast sea breeze and push inland. The sea \nbreeze has already formed this afternoon, mainly from Cape Canaveral \nsouthward. The sea breeze collision is forecast to occur along the I-\n95 corridor this afternoon/early evening. Storm motion will be light \nand north to northwesterly around 10 mph, which will bring activity \nback towards the coast and out into the Atlantic.  \n\nAn earlier start to convection today, as scattered showers and \nstorms formed across the west coast of Florida and moved into east \ncentral Florida through late morning. Additional storms have moved \ninto east central Florida and are currently heading toward the \neastern Coast, mainly from Cape Canaveral southward, with trailing \nstorms around Lake Okeechobee area.  Scattered to numerous showers \nand storms are forecast to form this afternoon (PoP 70-80%). Highest \ncoverage will be along the I-95 corridor this afternoon into early \nevening. An earlier start to convection today, with scattered \nshowers and storms moving across east central Florida, mainly \nKissimmee to Melbourne northward, late this morning through early \nthis afternoon. Forecast soundings show a moist profile, although \nnot as moist as it has been the past couple of days, with PW values \nof 2.0-2.3\". The environment supports convection, with plenty of \ninstability (MUCAPE of 1800-2200 J/kg), adequate buoyancy (lifted \nindex of -5 to -6), and sufficient downdraft potential (DCAPE 500-\n800 J/kg). Temperatures aloft are slightly warmer than they were \nyesterday (-6 to -6.5C at 500 mb). Because of this, main storm \nhazards will be frequent lightning, gusty winds of 40-50 mph, and \ntorrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a relatively short period of \ntime, promoting minor/nuisance flooding in urban and poor drainage \nareas. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has a Marginal (level 1 \nof 4) Risk for excessive rainfall for all of east central Florida \ntoday. \n\nSeasonable and humid today, with  afternoon highs in the upper 80s \nto low 90s. These temperatures combined with high humidity will \nproduce peak heat indices of 100-107F. Warm and muggy conditions \nwill continue overnight, with lows in the low to mid 70s. \n\nTuesday-Sunday... Upper level high pressure across the Great Plains \nwill shift eastward through mid week as the trough off the eastern \nUS coast shifts farther eastward, with the trough axis remaining \nover the central Florida through Tuesday. At the surface, a weak \nfrontal boundary across north Florida may push farther southward \ninto north-central to central Florida Tuesday before washing out. \nMostly diurnal convection through the period with fairly high rain \nchances through the period, 40-70% Tuesday, and 60-70% Wednesday-\nSunday. Storm motion will be fairly weak and erratic, however some \nactivity will periodically push back towards the coast, especially \nwith stronger boundary collisions. Storm impacts will be similar to \ntoday, with locally heavy rainfall continuing to be a concern.\nSeasonable temperatures and humid conditions are forecast each day, \nwith highs in the upper 80s to low 90s and heat indices of 100-107. \nWarm and muggy conditions will continue overnight, with lows in the \nlow to mid 70s.",
